Found by the B038 lane, 2026-08-30, while implementing the shadowed-variable
quick fix. Pre-existing; user-facing.

## Defect
`navigation::rename` (src/server/navigation.rs, ~:319) is a workspace-wide
TEXTUAL token match. Its own doc comment states it plainly:

    Uses the same token-scan strategy as `find_references` -- every `Ident`
    token in the open workspace whose text matches the cursor's bare name is
    rewritten.

There is no scope analysis, no declaration binding, and no member/qualifier
filtering. So invoking Rename Symbol on a local variable `x` rewrites:
- the outer binding `x` it shadows
- every unrelated local named `x` in every other function
- every unrelated global or parameter named `x`
- every member access `obj.x`
- both sides of `A::x`
- named-argument labels `f(x: 1)`

...in EVERY open file. Renaming a loop counter `i` in a real plugin is
catastrophic.

`rename_provider` is advertised as a live capability
(`OneOf::Left(true)` in the ServerCapabilities block), so VS Code offers
Rename Symbol (F2) and users will reasonably trust it.

## Why B038 did not fix it
B038's brief said to reuse `navigation::rename` for the shadowed-variable quick
fix. The lane refused, correctly: reusing it would have renamed the outer
binding, which is precisely what that fix must NOT do. It built a scope model
instead — `src/server/code_actions/fixes/rename_and_const/scope.rs` — and left
the live handler alone, because changing a live request handler was out of its
lane.

**The scope model to promote already exists and is tested.** From B038:
- occurrence set is `[declarator, innermost_scope_end)`, where the scope is the
  NARROWER of an AST statement scope and a token brace region — braces alone
  get `for`-init wrong (too wide), statements alone miss lambda bodies
- minus member names after `.`, both sides of `::`, named-argument labels, the
  declarator's own initialiser, and each nested redeclaration's range excluding
  its initialiser
- refuses outright on nested lambdas, same-scope redeclaration, and names that
  are also globals

## Work
- Promote that scope model into `navigation` (or a shared module) and make
  `textDocument/rename` use it.
- Decide what rename does for each symbol CLASS, since the scope model above is
  built for locals: parameters, class fields, methods, free functions,
  namespaced names, enum values, globals. A local's scope is lexical; a field's
  is the class plus every `obj.field` access; a free function's is the
  workspace. **Do not ship one strategy for all of them.**
- Where the correct rename cannot be determined, `textDocument/prepareRename`
  should REFUSE rather than let the user rename destructively. A refused
  rename is a papercut; a wrong one silently corrupts a plugin.
- Consider whether `find_references` has the same defect (the doc comment says
  rename uses "the same token-scan strategy"), and fix both or say why not.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: renaming a local `x` that shadows an outer `x` must not touch the outer
binding, and must not touch `obj.x`, `A::x`, a named-argument label `x:`, or a
same-named local in another function. Every one of those fails today.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- each symbol class: local, parameter, field, method, free function,
  namespaced function, enum value, global
- a local shadowing a parameter, a field, and a global
- rename inside a `for` init, a loop body, an unbraced `if` body, a `switch`
  case, and a lambda
- the new name colliding with something in scope -> refuse, do not shadow
- multi-file: a workspace symbol renames across files; a local does not leave
  its file
- a name appearing in a string literal or comment -> untouched
- multi-byte UTF-8 and CRLF sources -> ranges stay correct (LSP ranges are
  UTF-16)
- truncated / unbalanced source -> no panic (B038's fixture set covers five
  such shapes and can be reused)
- `prepareRename` refuses on a cursor that is not a renameable symbol

## Gate
FP fixtures byte-identical — rename is a request handler and adds no
diagnostics. Compare two binaries on the same machine at the same time.
`just test-hermetic` green.

## Related
B038 (merged) — built and tested the scope model this should promote, and
documented why it could not use the existing handler. B035 (the code-action
seam). B047 (the two code-action producer paths).

## Coordinator pre-dispatch findings, 2026-08-30

**`find_references` has the SAME defect — confirmed, not just suspected.**
`src/server/navigation.rs:364` is byte-for-byte the same strategy as `rename`:
tokenize every open file, keep every `Ident` whose text equals the bare name,
emit a Location. No scope, no binding, no member/qualifier filter. Fix both.
It is the read-only twin of the same bug: Find All References shows garbage,
and it is what a user checks BEFORE trusting a rename.

**`prepareRename` is not implemented at all.** There is no `prepare_rename`
function in `navigation.rs` (only `rename` at :319 and `find_references` at
:364), and `src/server/mod.rs:1132` advertises
`rename_provider: Some(OneOf::Left(true))` — the plain-bool form, which does
NOT advertise prepare support. So the client never gets a chance to be told
'this is not renameable'; F2 goes straight to the destructive path. The
ticket's 'prepareRename should REFUSE' therefore means ADDING the handler and
switching that field to `OneOf::Right(RenameOptions { prepare_provider:
Some(true), .. })`.

**Lane-sequencing consequence:** that one-line change puts B049 in the
ServerCapabilities block of `src/server/mod.rs`, which B040 is currently
rewriting (~900 lines) and which B039 and B041 also need. B049 is dispatched
anyway because the substance of its work is `navigation.rs` plus a promoted
scope module, which nobody else touches; the capability edit is last and
minimal, and B049 rebases onto post-B040 master before its final gates.
B039 / B041 / B049 must not hold that block concurrently.